cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Res-Lens: a lens on the residual stream — and a scalpel for it"
message: "If you use or modify any part of this software, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Salehi Ashtiani"
    given-names: "Kourosh"
    email: "kourosh.salehi.ashtiani@gmail.com"
repository-code: "https://github.com/kouroshSA/Res-Lens"
license-url: "https://github.com/kouroshSA/Res-Lens/blob/main/LICENSE.md"
abstract: >-
  Tools for locating where a decoder-only transformer makes a binary decision in
  its residual stream, and causally testing whether that decision depends on a
  single direction — by ablating the direction out of the weights (necessity) and
  by steering along it (sufficiency), each with matched random-direction controls.
keywords:
  - interpretability
  - residual stream
  - directional ablation
  - activation steering
  - protein language model
  - nanoGPT
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Refusal in language models is mediated by a single direction"
    authors:
      - family-names: Arditi
        given-names: Andy
